  'Understand the Constitution as text, structure, history, practice, and contested method of legal interpretation. Topics cover the Supreme Court and case method, judicial review and justiciability, congressional power, federalism, separation of powers, executive power, state action and incorporation, due process, equal protection, the First Amendment, religion clauses, the Fourth Amendment and constitutional policing, takings and economic liberties, and contemporary constitutional issues.',
  $clawjson${"slug": "constitutional-law", "title": "Constitutional Law", "description": "Understand the Constitution as text, structure, history, practice, and contested method of legal interpretation. Topics cover the Supreme Court and case method, judicial review and justiciability, congressional power, federalism, separation of powers, executive power, state action and incorporation, due process, equal protection, the First Amendment, religion clauses, the Fourth Amendment and constitutional policing, takings and economic liberties, and contemporary constitutional issues.", "topics": [{"id": "6b466092-0032-442d-83d2-e6fb24af3a36", "type": "topic", "title": "Constitutional Foundations, Text, History, and Interpretation", "description": "Understand the Constitution as text, structure, history, practice, and contested method of legal interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "6571e690-d7cb-48a1-8542-d5f7860f7f63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional text and structure", "children": [{"id": "9486ad84-11d3-4b99-8044-1fe69417159c", "type": "concept", "title": "Articles I-III"}, {"id": "4e1aeb65-10aa-4ce8-9af8-a7ae86626532", "type": "concept", "title": "Article IV and federal union"}, {"id": "7957c8e9-9919-41af-9964-e35a4dc90798", "type": "concept", "title": "Article VI supremacy"}, {"id": "72e4b8dc-8425-4bbb-ac0d-12962749c7ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Bill of Rights and Reconstruction Amendments"}], "description": "This subtopic grounds students in the Constitution's architecture before they study doctrine."}, {"id": "707a2569-abf5-4674-a3a3-d70ff6d5f65c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical foundations", "children": [{"id": "aa150c33-501a-4101-8c23-741083d92ff3", "type": "concept", "title": "Founding debates"}, {"id": "a6460b1e-a404-4029-ab96-f3d4fa307a29", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalist and Anti-Federalist arguments"}, {"id": "cfac512d-3ce5-467a-baef-23b29182c344", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil War and Reconstruction"}, {"id": "042a870e-1b94-445e-b907-900a1e266a8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Progressive and modern constitutional change"}], "description": "Historical context explains why constitutional choices were made and why later amendments transformed the original structure."}, {"id": "d24d0353-5b8f-452c-bef1-e3b7c56df9d0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive methods", "children": [{"id": "67acd606-59cf-4eb8-8db9-9c6db8f2decb", "type": "concept", "title": "Textualism"}, {"id": "74f1998d-a9a2-47a8-88ef-23dbe3e467fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Originalism"}, {"id": "5c06b14b-bc07-4fab-ad7a-0dd60c16af93", "type": "concept", "title": "Living constitutionalism"}, {"id": "40c59e27-11ae-4598-92a6-b1e290e6361d", "type": "concept", "title": "Precedent and historical practice"}], "description": "Interpretive methods shape how judges, lawyers, and scholars argue from text to rule."}]}, {"id": "3279fa2d-cf0b-4693-beeb-d0c9739baa4c", "type": "topic", "title": "Supreme Court Role, Case Method, Precedent, and Constitutional Argument", "description": "Learn how constitutional doctrine is built through cases, reasoning, precedent, facts, holdings, and competing opinions.", "children": [{"id": "b9e97acc-1088-406f-b5a6-ee84f5b6f4b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Supreme Court institutions", "children": [{"id": "b23b50e9-76a7-4b78-b1f2-ed74612a4b55", "type": "concept", "title": "Certiorari"}, {"id": "f1fdac63-d20b-49f0-b9f8-fa0e1017354d", "type": "concept", "title": "Briefing and oral argument"}, {"id": "b937fb18-0390-483f-acc8-16dedb307a93", "type": "concept", "title": "Majority, concurrence, and dissent"}, {"id": "3c45b4a6-ea33-4c5a-b65b-efd2861896fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Remedies and mandates"}], "description": "Students learn how the Court fits into the broader constitutional system."}, {"id": "4801da76-633f-4177-9a12-a2002f955ae1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Case method", "children": [{"id": "f95fdafb-2ba5-4fd8-be94-c7e51f87af34", "type": "concept", "title": "Facts"}, {"id": "032af68d-b5c3-499f-8a63-91f5527e341c", "type": "concept", "title": "Issue"}, {"id": "40f725e7-6315-46fb-b1a9-64afe642acb0", "type": "concept", "title": "Holding"}, {"id": "6273d375-0c9e-46b9-964e-94f3c1bab178", "type": "concept", "title": "Reasoning"}], "description": "Case method teaches students to extract rules and reasoning from judicial opinions rather than memorize outcomes."}, {"id": "55fdcc69-f381-44de-a74f-fb266f27c236", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional argument", "children": [{"id": "2079a4b8-1212-4046-9402-dcbfadd2ccc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Rule synthesis"}, {"id": "4c8318a0-8998-4e60-bfb2-3baaf2d6a466", "type": "concept", "title": "Analogical reasoning"}, {"id": "302c4ad5-65fd-4a56-9c1d-a0ad1dce4db7", "type": "concept", "title": "Distinguishing cases"}, {"id": "22afd881-56c7-4270-8035-159d9faeea14", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing constitutional values"}], "description": "World-class constitutional study requires seeing how advocates combine text, history, precedent, policy, and institutional consequences."}]}, {"id": "d000409b-50d1-48cd-b01f-75d523f3647d", "type": "topic", "title": "Judicial Review, Article III, and Justiciability", "description": "Study the power and limits of courts, including judicial review, standing, ripeness, mootness, political questions, and remedies.", "children": [{"id": "c7ef8002-a173-48b0-9d72-58e3a5ca4a4a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judicial review", "children": [{"id": "2cfc842a-49d7-4ead-99f0-459ea7a2af66", "type": "concept", "title": "Marbury v. Madison"}, {"id": "3a607b22-7619-4d30-b50b-9ca9a90e7bdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional supremacy"}, {"id": "546865d7-8340-4d5b-86b4-38404f651470", "type": "concept", "title": "Departmentalism"}, {"id": "eb3ce754-54e5-4bcd-bb70-6a76859f7a5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Countermajoritarian difficulty"}], "description": "Judicial review raises the central question of who gets to say what the Constitution means."}, {"id": "7d9aa35c-bd7a-470b-bfed-726d66684185", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Article III limits", "children": [{"id": "3e17b9d7-8e78-4ff3-ad9e-cf46acb0bac7", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing"}, {"id": "9fca2783-ac9f-4f71-b37f-c411bd9be1c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Injury in fact"}, {"id": "46c8aa7c-2147-4973-b6f2-f480b5408e7c", "type": "concept", "title": "Causation and redressability"}, {"id": "9e5d7476-d58f-4431-a49c-dd7ad1881dda", "type": "concept", "title": "Advisory opinion prohibition"}], "description": "Article III limits keep federal courts tied to cases and controversies."}, {"id": "78803e99-4bc7-4474-a3f2-7e0386fa0c8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Justiciability doctrines", "children": [{"id": "3c409688-6245-4a9d-a462-ca3e7ba43014", "type": "concept", "title": "Ripeness"}, {"id": "81aa22cc-3530-4f71-a649-d6a99c9a6d4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Mootness"}, {"id": "4036d585-dd63-4de4-ac4b-5be97937f659", "type": "concept", "title": "Political question doctrine"}, {"id": "4838654d-cce0-4f8b-873e-bb400e6535b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Prudential limits"}], "description": "Justiciability doctrines determine when constitutional questions can be heard in court."}]}, {"id": "6b30366d-363e-4f49-b6f2-a329ce2a13b5", "type": "topic", "title": "Congressional Power: Commerce, Taxing, Spending, and Necessary and Proper", "description": "Analyze the constitutional basis and limits of federal legislative power.", "children": [{"id": "a0873803-0fc7-4501-b0ec-3f591378d1db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Commerce Clause", "children": [{"id": "95586c89-5228-4dc4-86dc-f00e2dc2c51d", "type": "concept", "title": "Interstate commerce"}, {"id": "10ad0337-9e18-4ca2-862e-257a4f5b6a5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Substantial effects"}, {"id": "fa36f4b1-ec6d-44d9-ad09-08849f1c43d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Activity/inactivity debates"}, {"id": "cb4e8277-5e61-422f-be71-000c7de62b1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Modern limits"}], "description": "Commerce doctrine tracks the changing boundary between national economic regulation and state authority."}, {"id": "2ba2314e-2e84-46a3-ad87-6775f6669c14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taxing and spending powers", "children": [{"id": "54866b56-d1aa-4012-96c8-ed1ef0deef74", "type": "concept", "title": "Taxing power"}, {"id": "0b7b324a-e99b-4cfb-98fb-5644b34bd7db", "type": "concept", "title": "Spending conditions"}, {"id": "4667ab70-a86b-4f21-b84b-9ebf14ca2d39", "type": "concept", "title": "Coercion concerns"}, {"id": "707f4fe9-1ba5-4b7a-89cf-333adb55f3fa", "type": "concept", "title": "General welfare debates"}], "description": "Congress can influence national policy through revenue and conditional funding."}, {"id": "a238f34c-9dbb-4e7c-a392-181356117cbd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Necessary and Proper Clause", "children": [{"id": "a8c207db-dfcb-438b-a1ec-c3e0ee73fdbb", "type": "concept", "title": "McCulloch v. Maryland"}, {"id": "138fe1e6-c92a-4ef2-a57d-0b7131f7bf1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Implied powers"}, {"id": "91a9b20b-1e82-49e3-bf5e-c63cfbc50256", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational means"}, {"id": "dd7c45c0-92a4-4d61-9ad7-ef3bd5c5d3d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism constraints"}], "description": "Necessary and Proper reasoning explains implied powers and means-end analysis."}]}, {"id": "a9a19eef-f699-4b82-8f95-7a2717ed609a", "type": "topic", "title": "Federalism, State Power, Preemption, and the Dormant Commerce Clause", "description": "Examine the allocation of authority between national and state governments and the constitutional limits on state regulation.", "children": [{"id": "eaf23342-a4f6-4d56-8c5d-78ba51268a27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism principles", "children": [{"id": "71b995f1-72c9-48a0-b3a3-c0c9cc2fac2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Enumerated powers"}, {"id": "2f9e7535-388b-463b-a132-0622f8104584", "type": "concept", "title": "Reserved powers"}, {"id": "15ca3f6f-9c51-46ec-b0b5-79e3172058d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Tenth Amendment"}, {"id": "248bfb56-0daf-4d72-95b4-3aed17008bdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-commandeering"}], "description": "Federalism allocates power, protects local variation, and creates recurring conflicts over national authority."}, {"id": "ff65a2ce-3f01-4ffb-ac15-630fb1c6e809", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Supremacy and preemption", "children": [{"id": "ec1773e0-5692-4383-ae9c-f5846bd7faf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Express preemption"}, {"id": "c9b609ed-c190-4df4-bdd7-f8facfc638de", "type": "concept", "title": "Field preemption"}, {"id": "9014c661-9e06-4a87-9b03-d58deb8743a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict preemption"}, {"id": "525d815e-09a3-4856-8464-d7109fe24786", "type": "concept", "title": "Obstacle preemption"}], "description": "Preemption determines when federal law displaces state law."}, {"id": "24ba3ea9-94eb-4827-8577-97cfbc8d9198", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dormant Commerce Clause", "children": [{"id": "04fd5e90-bb82-4d4f-bfb0-694a3ab0d6ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Discrimination against interstate commerce"}, {"id": "6764164c-54c8-43b2-af59-ee4bebecc484", "type": "concept", "title": "Pike balancing"}, {"id": "1afe1c11-a4a9-4834-b6c8-97ad8ee73175", "type": "concept", "title": "Market participant exception"}, {"id": "30885556-b788-4dea-b992-5c402241ec71", "type": "concept", "title": "State police powers"}], "description": "The Dormant Commerce Clause limits state laws that burden interstate commerce even without federal legislation."}]}, {"id": "e1f73772-47df-443a-b7cd-29bf38623c81", "type": "topic", "title": "Separation of Powers, Legislative Power, and Administrative Authority", "description": "Explore checks and balances, delegation, agency power, and institutional conflict among branches.", "children": [{"id": "46232802-fe53-42f3-a7db-b9068f9cf6a5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Checks and balances", "children": [{"id": "a76edee6-8ff2-4ef7-aacb-a388ab603df2", "type": "concept", "title": "Veto"}, {"id": "3efb9f06-cd79-41a0-a062-f65d0431093a", "type": "concept", "title": "Appointments"}, {"id": "956f1997-1376-4cc8-bea7-f831a3244827", "type": "concept", "title": "Impeachment"}, {"id": "17284c3d-8eb4-4e18-b782-166b7986da5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Oversight"}], "description": "Separation of powers is not pure separation; it is a design of shared and contested authority."}, {"id": "313e4e7d-fe9d-4ce8-9e76-d3aa7d50a59a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legislative power and delegation", "children": [{"id": "774c069e-fa71-4eb8-ad58-dc80a4a61eef", "type": "concept", "title": "Nondelegation doctrine"}, {"id": "c05f2e00-acff-43ca-a19b-7f3a58eb99df", "type": "concept", "title": "Intelligible principle"}, {"id": "04e5cb7d-16f3-420f-a106-44d2c4f9b125", "type": "concept", "title": "Major questions doctrine"}, {"id": "001ac6b3-e8c5-4cf9-96c7-16cee71e5242", "type": "concept", "title": "Legislative veto limits"}], "description": "Delegation doctrine addresses how much policymaking authority Congress may give agencies."}, {"id": "541897f2-d771-43d8-a7bb-bc2eb9a4d3db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Administrative authority", "children": [{"id": "79a342f7-9de3-418a-aad4-5e4892936108", "type": "concept", "title": "Agency rulemaking"}, {"id": "4475bc92-d74f-4a96-824b-0182a57720f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Agency adjudication"}, {"id": "35329ee6-7edc-4a50-a49e-851b06066db2", "type": "concept", "title": "Chevron/Loper Bright-era questions"}, {"id": "d47f645f-5629-490c-b277-261da81ff6d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability and expertise"}], "description": "Administrative power connects constitutional structure to modern governance."}]}, {"id": "09d5356a-3f2d-471e-9f6e-2dca655d0e19", "type": "topic", "title": "Executive Power, Foreign Affairs, War Powers, and Emergency Authority", "description": "Evaluate presidential power in domestic, foreign affairs, national security, and emergency contexts.", "children": [{"id": "320b902c-1dee-41b6-806b-7fa82792e800", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Executive power framework", "children": [{"id": "e2da6e3d-7d75-4c40-92c1-8c3adc50c18f", "type": "concept", "title": "Article II"}, {"id": "66a19573-1eaa-4e0e-ac8a-792948d2ee61", "type": "concept", "title": "Youngstown framework"}, {"id": "da88e676-4d01-4723-8fb9-af2be33277b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Take Care Clause"}, {"id": "918072e2-04f9-47b9-bd87-93ae3e704e1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Unitary executive debates"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze presidential authority by source, context, and conflict with Congress."}, {"id": "74baa582-ebcc-4fea-a08f-12d9171a0c21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Foreign affairs and war powers", "children": [{"id": "4f437546-64eb-4d27-a738-93e2f1a18185", "type": "concept", "title": "Commander in Chief"}, {"id": "c4f488d1-7eb0-45ef-ab55-b2c1098cc40f", "type": "concept", "title": "Treaties and executive agreements"}, {"id": "392f175a-6027-4aca-ab46-d82f3bcb7097", "type": "concept", "title": "War Powers Resolution"}, {"id": "95e11d2e-5c17-4735-9abf-9bea391d4c94", "type": "concept", "title": "Foreign affairs preemption"}], "description": "Foreign affairs doctrine tests the boundaries between executive initiative and legislative control."}, {"id": "c79ec005-27ad-42ad-8447-a208e3c9e4f7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Emergency authority", "children": [{"id": "bfef89a6-5899-4182-b4e5-556e579cbcae", "type": "concept", "title": "National emergencies"}, {"id": "ef9cf03d-c1a9-4c4c-9a9b-eda7eb5de01f", "type": "concept", "title": "Habeas and detention"}, {"id": "6a5f2b92-32ba-4858-9e2d-1a743881af48", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic emergency power"}, {"id": "5cc25046-e327-4e43-ba0c-a3637df21089", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial deference"}], "description": "Emergency cases reveal how constitutional law responds to crisis, security, and civil liberties."}]}, {"id": "f3c80560-5797-47eb-a00c-e5554aa7942b", "type": "topic", "title": "State Action, Incorporation, and the Fourteenth Amendment Framework", "description": "Understand when constitutional rights bind governments and how the Bill of Rights became applicable to states.", "children": [{"id": "eecfb5ee-9561-48fa-81db-229cd1752c41", "type": "subtopic", "title": "State action doctrine", "children": [{"id": "afd3bb5b-0dfb-4c0f-a6f3-1fa46972909b", "type": "concept", "title": "Public function"}, {"id": "138189e6-04c9-4281-827d-c23ce5432ef6", "type": "concept", "title": "Entanglement"}, {"id": "f74bcffd-e26f-4e24-89de-b7ab0658bff4", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbiotic relationship"}, {"id": "d90d1eba-1312-475c-8c4b-f52b8e8b28d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Private discrimination limits"}], "description": "State action determines when constitutional limits apply to conduct involving private actors."}, {"id": "6cd68c22-3dc1-4a45-9099-6371aa22091a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Incorporation", "children": [{"id": "a1d75c4e-6ed4-41e5-a8ab-262126c0e712", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment"}, {"id": "06786c5b-1599-4ee2-b336-a382c810a896", "type": "concept", "title": "Selective incorporation"}, {"id": "68902354-bf3e-4671-8981-98b0d0b260c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Due Process Clause"}, {"id": "10943f1f-711a-4e6f-8e66-388d44e4e058", "type": "concept", "title": "Privileges or Immunities debates"}], "description": "Incorporation explains how most Bill of Rights protections became enforceable against states."}, {"id": "18d995be-838f-4bed-b9c4-69b3a1e1f9ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reconstruction constitutionalism", "children": [{"id": "231da7c4-f437-43c8-bbcc-968f758cc62d", "type": "concept", "title": "Thirteenth Amendment"}, {"id": "22bf7a95-b24e-4c83-b8cb-53e4f3fc32a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourteenth Amendment"}, {"id": "4f9fecc3-a5e6-4b0b-9f8f-e352fb5c3806", "type": "concept", "title": "Fifteenth Amendment"}, {"id": "5a2a2115-ab58-409d-9b45-ed380b83fc45", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional enforcement power"}], "description": "The Reconstruction Amendments transformed federal-state relations and civil-rights enforcement."}]}, {"id": "e3833bc2-94e8-485a-b461-789dace2db60", "type": "topic", "title": "Due Process: Procedural, Substantive, Privacy, and Fundamental Rights", "description": "Distinguish procedural fairness from substantive rights and analyze privacy, autonomy, and liberty doctrines.", "children": [{"id": "5e846fd2-ebd1-4f27-8e68-1b16b0a9ac4c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Procedural due process", "children": [{"id": "3bee6a38-16b9-45d4-bad4-1f5884cba8f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Protected interests"}, {"id": "2fd2073f-b864-4c38-a2f6-7050fa3c312c", "type": "concept", "title": "Notice and hearing"}, {"id": "0b143f46-96aa-4e8c-8873-db9581b8d831", "type": "concept", "title": "Mathews balancing"}, {"id": "3b7e57f9-a629-4336-88a1-67d3c10bf056", "type": "concept", "title": "Government benefits and discipline"}], "description": "Procedural due process asks what process government owes before depriving life, liberty, or property."}, {"id": "5f686ec7-d651-44b5-87a9-e8d0134aae57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Substantive due process", "children": [{"id": "612648f8-f173-4173-bdd0-6c57b86068dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental rights"}, {"id": "bd88b130-95aa-4099-9ab1-8ef07ad56613", "type": "concept", "title": "History and tradition"}, {"id": "e164f6ed-d4f7-44cb-a021-cb177cc53de2", "type": "concept", "title": "Strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "ad9367ea-659c-4224-85aa-72b916bbe1d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis review"}], "description": "Substantive due process asks whether some liberties are protected from government interference regardless of procedure."}, {"id": "f369c12e-bc4d-45ee-b1c6-97b91afe81e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Privacy and autonomy", "children": [{"id": "168c4d67-5497-4821-b5df-052d0dbd19e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage"}, {"id": "46f084e7-a90d-418c-ae3f-a2727e17ab39", "type": "concept", "title": "Family and parenting"}, {"id": "85763391-1031-48f4-a771-e8fa568d4fb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual intimacy"}, {"id": "3ac1f554-4ac9-444f-8d81-b61ed25e9fd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Reproductive liberty and post-Dobbs doctrine"}], "description": "Privacy doctrine shows the contested relationship between liberty, family, bodily integrity, and democratic regulation."}]}, {"id": "48e0fdd3-560f-4e1c-8fb5-64ebe0b22fb6", "type": "topic", "title": "Equal Protection: Scrutiny, Race, Gender, and Civil Rights", "description": "Apply equal protection doctrine to classifications, government purposes, tiers of scrutiny, and civil-rights struggles.", "children": [{"id": "03f012ce-5035-4782-82df-1c4354e9cb43", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scrutiny framework", "children": [{"id": "4dada6cd-524b-4a24-a519-bcfc35114a66", "type": "concept", "title": "Rational basis"}, {"id": "60cc3c08-0c80-487d-9cef-4304103e1cf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Intermediate scrutiny"}, {"id": "1600d25f-c69f-4f23-bf30-7be1b21552d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Strict scrutiny"}, {"id": "e0b07751-6844-4ed8-8b9a-24cba603ab03", "type": "concept", "title": "Burden shifting"}], "description": "Scrutiny levels organize how courts evaluate classifications and government justifications."}, {"id": "7bd49022-4831-49eb-abbd-da9a650e3831", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and suspect classifications", "children": [{"id": "c0d5b255-53c0-4ec1-bad2-8177eafe1e0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Brown v. Board"}, {"id": "ed95d9b6-0083-4897-802f-c0340960d09c", "type": "concept", "title": "Discriminatory purpose"}, {"id": "29671f46-6810-47e6-8560-58ea75d6a1ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Facial vs disparate impact"}, {"id": "bc7d8748-6298-40b2-93ad-5c37190a9f2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Affirmative action limits"}], "description": "Race doctrine connects constitutional equality to slavery, segregation, affirmative action, and voting."}, {"id": "d1eb3b60-19a7-4b92-b479-f147c971dec6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender and other classifications", "children": [{"id": "4df16dd6-1312-4b95-b65e-f400e402c96a", "type": "concept", "title": "Intermediate scrutiny"}, {"id": "84d147ae-e015-40fc-93b9-d29cde2d2d64", "type": "concept", "title": "Stereotyping"}, {"id": "1888c461-3945-409f-89d5-0da26726b79c", "type": "concept", "title": "Animus doctrine"}, {"id": "541e0f13-b40b-4bc1-8192-3d062eccda4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Fundamental interests plus classification"}], "description": "Equal protection doctrine also addresses gender, sexual orientation, legitimacy, disability, age, and poverty."}]}, {"id": "c623f160-10ab-414c-9a93-db9616397909", "type": "topic", "title": "First Amendment: Speech, Press, Association, and Digital Public Square", "description": "Study free expression doctrine, content discrimination, public forums, press freedom, association, and modern speech problems.", "children": [{"id": "e3fee6a4-2fd2-4162-8170-1703ec54388b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Speech regulation frameworks", "children": [{"id": "e75ead41-39cf-4824-9d32-c92fbfb4a6d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Content-based regulation"}, {"id": "7c7eb391-7e97-469c-a156-bad46408283e", "type": "concept", "title": "Content-neutral regulation"}, {"id": "519bfa98-345c-44ff-ba2e-38cfddce70ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Prior restraint"}, {"id": "f4cbcca0-9663-42dd-aa42-b69f818d5c69", "type": "concept", "title": "Overbreadth and vagueness"}], "description": "Free speech doctrine distinguishes content-based restrictions from time, place, and manner rules."}, {"id": "3e1af83a-8b08-445a-8b23-0582e5c275fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forums and special contexts", "children": [{"id": "bc2e09e3-cefe-4c5c-ae71-308d2bf1e778", "type": "concept", "title": "Public forum doctrine"}, {"id": "6d74ed79-5f6a-4d2a-b0de-f914baeef66c", "type": "concept", "title": "Schools"}, {"id": "a1c1dac3-3b2e-432f-8739-1b767d947064", "type": "concept", "title": "Public employees"}, {"id": "8e6fcc43-6423-40aa-91dd-59576ff4dd0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Campaign finance and compelled speech"}], "description": "Speech rights vary by context, speaker, and government role."}, {"id": "cb6a7080-dea7-4013-a516-889de1526a48", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Press, association, and digital speech", "children": [{"id": "40a6a079-21f1-4d8f-beca-021ac06682ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Press freedom"}, {"id": "b34932ee-45a6-4d3d-a487-ef529004e888", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom of association"}, {"id": "8dc43215-efd7-420d-ac69-3b7c4f3c51f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Social media and platforms"}, {"id": "07f9e9ec-7822-4dab-9d04-470a0a75d52e", "type": "concept", "title": "Incitement, threats, and misinformation debates"}], "description": "Modern First Amendment problems involve media, platforms, protest, association, and online public discourse."}]}, {"id": "763c0cca-b94f-404c-a19f-09360443d220", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion Clauses: Establishment and Free Exercise", "description": "Analyze how constitutional law regulates government involvement with religion and protects religious exercise.", "children": [{"id": "0e1c6860-7772-4081-8e1a-bd40d086b780", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Establishment Clause", "children": [{"id": "14e9d027-143c-4f40-b971-6fd4cbde3162", "type": "concept", "title": "Coercion"}, {"id": "3df0cb1f-b7d6-4d6f-9d60-87a359f4191d", "type": "concept", "title": "Endorsement and history tests"}, {"id": "969d1632-10c9-4ea4-af9b-3ec19c661edb", "type": "concept", "title": "Government funding"}, {"id": "93598b0d-7d28-403e-8d87-a273a51039b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Public schools"}], "description": "Establishment doctrine limits government action that supports, coerces, or entangles itself with religion."}, {"id": "d9d12ce6-2149-4899-8019-a46d687f6f90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Free Exercise Clause", "children": [{"id": "5f9c569b-896c-4e60-835a-3a08c9613e15", "type": "concept", "title": "Neutral laws of general applicability"}, {"id": "34615338-4cc2-4116-bfe3-35deb0a5fb69", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious exemptions"}, {"id": "2e9ebd3d-39f9-4660-ad80-075eec3d3aff", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid rights debates"}, {"id": "ed9aa74b-4e10-4c1f-aa48-31ee627a27be", "type": "concept", "title": "Substantial burden"}], "description": "Free exercise doctrine protects religious practice while interacting with neutral laws and exemptions."}, {"id": "f10afb29-8d87-494e-badf-339f6ec7b130", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Religion in public institutions", "children": [{"id": "ca5f438b-4784-4406-94ae-da2da54bacea", "type": "concept", "title": "Prayer and religious expression"}, {"id": "37c8fb64-737c-485c-8a98-e1e0bcbc6ef8", "type": "concept", "title": "Religious organizations"}, {"id": "b9e11e12-609b-4c9c-9198-849b1cb6565a", "type": "concept", "title": "Conscience claims"}, {"id": "486d0d84-fb93-482a-a15e-ee99a6b960d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Equality and religion conflicts"}], "description": "Religion cases often arise in schools, prisons, workplaces, funding programs, and public ceremonies."}]}, {"id": "54f53566-f947-4982-9e96-c49d279ae1cf", "type": "topic", "title": "Fourth Amendment, Criminal Procedure, and Constitutional Policing", "description": "Connect constitutional rights to search, seizure, privacy, exclusion, due process, and criminal justice practice.", "children": [{"id": "44a115c0-42e0-4f66-97b5-85fd8e555b10", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Search and seizure foundations", "children": [{"id": "7e8b8f22-3bb5-425d-8dbb-f76627f690bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Reasonable expectation of privacy"}, {"id": "b073b872-eb59-4ff4-8dfe-150f7eb5f1e6", "type": "concept", "title": "Property-based approach"}, {"id": "01a3d2ac-ff24-44ef-ab31-b2ab5d10edf7", "type": "concept", "title": "Probable cause"}, {"id": "76d7e932-94dc-4aec-931f-ec96c491482e", "type": "concept", "title": "Warrants"}], "description": "Fourth Amendment doctrine defines when government investigation becomes constitutionally regulated."}, {"id": "9a19a724-cba3-43c4-8f62-33062859c11e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Exceptions and remedies", "children": [{"id": "781ba